{"id":"references/Petersen/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch05_GeodesicsAndDistance#def:pet-ch5-exponential-map","title":"def:pet-ch5-exponential-map","path":"references/Petersen/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch05_GeodesicsAndDistance.md","line":526,"body":"","kind":"definition","status":"not_started","tex":"references/Petersen/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch05_GeodesicsAndDistance.tex#def:pet-ch5-exponential-map","uses":["[[references/Petersen/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch05_GeodesicsAndDistance#def:pet-ch5-geodesic]]"],"inspired_by":[],"bindings":[],"is_index":false,"body_html":"","formal":[],"tex_content":{"path":"references/Petersen/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch05_GeodesicsAndDistance.tex","label":"def:pet-ch5-exponential-map","line":1058,"source":"\\begin{definition}[exponential map; homogeneity property]\n  \\label{def:pet-ch5-exponential-map}\n\n  \\source{petersen:page-0204}\n  \\uses{def:pet-ch5-geodesic}\n\n  For $v\\in T_pM$ let $c_v$ be the unique geodesic with $c_v(0)=p$, $\\dot c_v(0)=v$,\n  and $[0,L_v)$ the nonnegative part of its maximal domain. Uniqueness of geodesics\n  gives the \\emph{homogeneity property} $c_{\\alpha v}(t)=c_v(\\alpha t)$ for\n  $\\alpha>0$, $t<L_{\\alpha v}$, so $L_{\\alpha v}=\\alpha^{-1}L_v$. Let $O_p\\subset\n  T_pM$ be the vectors $v$ with $1<L_v$. The \\emph{exponential map} at $p$,\n  $\\exp_p:O_p\\to M$, is $\\exp_p(v)=c_v(1)$; equivalently $\\exp_p(tv)=c_v(t)$, so\n  $\\exp_p(v)$ is reached from $p$ by going ``distance'' $|v|$ in direction $v/|v|$.\n  Collecting the $\\exp_p$ over $p\\in M$ gives $\\exp:O=\\bigcup_pO_p\\to M$. By\n  \\cref{lem:pet-ch5-uniform-neighborhood} and\n  \\cref{thm:pet-ch5-local-existence}, $O$ is open in\n  $TM$ and $\\exp$ is smooth; likewise each $O_p\\subset T_pM$ is open and $\\exp_p$ is\n  smooth.\n\\end{definition}","html":" <p></p><section class=\"theorem\"><div class=\"theorem-label\">Definition</div></section>[exponential map; homogeneity property] <span class=\"tex-label\" id=\"tex-label-6465663a7065742d6368352